## ls -t (sort by time)

Default sort is alphabetical.

**Three timestamps**

| Name | Updated when |
|------|--------------|
| `atime` | file is accessed (read) |
| `mtime` | file **content** changes |
| `ctime` | **metadata** changes (permissions, owner, location) |

Timestamp = seconds since the **Unix epoch** (midnight, 1 Jan 1970).

```bash
date
date +%s
```
Current date and time / the same as a timestamp.

```bash
ls -lt
```
Sort by mtime, newest first.

```bash
ls -ltu
```
Sort by and show atime.

```bash
ls -ltc
```
Sort by and show ctime.

**Experiment** (in a scratch folder, not the notes repo)
```bash
mkdir ~/scratch && cd ~/scratch
touch file-01 file-02
touch theNewestFile              # create a file
echo "hello world!" > file-02    # change content
chmod 444 file-01                # change permissions
```
Run `ls -ltu` and `ls -ltc` after each step.

| Action | mtime | ctime |
|--------|-------|-------|
| change content | yes | yes |
| change permissions | no | yes |

